From 5e7599176ebf2309108017d79231b9e9d891d02a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:53:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 22/25] WebRender: draw a tile that shows a stereo picture once
 per eye

A stereo picture that is not given a compositor surface of its own (an
opacity on the element, a turn, a clip the compositor cannot take) is
drawn into the page's picture cache tiles, where it showed both views
squeezed into its place. A tile of the root picture cache that draws
such a picture straight into itself is now drawn once per eye, when the
compositor shows such tiles (CompositorCapabilities::
supports_stereo_tiles): into a buffer twice as wide as the tile, the
left eye's drawing over the tile's valid rect and the right eye's beside
it, offset by the valid rect's width; every other primitive the same in
both. Each eye's drawing shows that eye's view over the picture's place
(a brush segment over the view's place, its texels the view's part of
the picture). The compositor declares the tile's surface two views full
side by side, left eye first (NativeLayerWaylandRender: buffers twice as
wide, the right eye's part read back with the left's, a tile visible
across part of its width copied side by side).

How a picture holds its views is the browser's decision, the same for a
picture with a surface of its own (layers::DecideStereo through
libstereo-policy): WebRender asks it through a policy the embedder
registers (webrender::set_stereo_policy), with the picture's mark and the
shape its place is shown at (a video's image is laid out at the frame's
size and scaled by a reference frame). DMABuf textures now carry their
stereo mark to WebRender as other textures do. Images that hold two views
are drawn through the brush path, which draws one eye's view; the quad
path draws a picture whole.

Views the browser drew itself (WebXR, the camera hack) hold one scene:
drawn once, in a surface the compositor cannot show per eye (a filter),
they show the left eye's view instead of both views squeezed together.
Other pictures drawn once show as they are, as before.

Off-screen passes (filters, masks) are drawn once.

